WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.480
So Frequency Lock Encoding is a very simple idea,
but it's just that: what if we intentionally inject
frequency impairments, so, subtle frequency shifts,
as a way of encoding data?

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:18.350
And this is really powerful because now we can
actually have another signaling layer, but we're
not using any extra airtime, because we're just
modulating the already existing signal.

3
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:23.550
We're not using any extra frequency bands. We're just
jittering the waveform ever so slightly.

4
00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:31.410
And we're not using any additional hardware
or antennas or things like that. We're just
modifying within the pull-in range of
whatever synchronizer.

5
00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:37.150
So we call this idea a subprotocol. So we have a
protocol living on top of another protocol.

6
00:00:37.150 --> 00:00:42.680
And so we can imagine a lot of different
topologies, but the one that we care about
is we can have a transmitter.

7
00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:50.400
And they're transmitting via their primary
protocol to a receiver, but they're also
transmitting to a secondary receiver
through this subprotocol.
